WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.312
Hi, I'm Pumice!

00:00:02.312 --> 00:00:05.037
Microsoft AI just finished pre-training MAI Base Two.

00:00:05.037 --> 00:00:08.783
Here is how it works, and whether its numbers add up.

00:00:10.142 --> 00:00:14.226
The trick is making expert imbalance cheap: it costs a little time,

00:00:14.226 --> 00:00:15.927
never memory or dropped tokens.

00:00:19.342 --> 00:00:23.167
Each token is routed to seven of five hundred and twelve experts,

00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:24.441
plus one shared expert.

00:00:24.861 --> 00:00:28.470
Experts sit in groups, each with a fixed number of token slots,

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:29.673
so memory never changes.

00:00:30.093 --> 00:00:33.093
A hot expert borrows empty slots from its neighbours.

00:00:33.093 --> 00:00:35.426
What still overflows runs in another round.

00:00:35.846 --> 00:00:37.872
Nothing is dropped and nothing crashes.

00:00:37.872 --> 00:00:40.573
A load balancing loss still nudges the router.

00:00:42.133 --> 00:00:46.385
In round zero every group has tokens, and communication overlaps the compute.

00:00:46.805 --> 00:00:49.043
In round one only two groups overflow.

00:00:49.043 --> 00:00:51.920
The round count is global, so the third waits.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.528
Muon needs whole matrices, but the momentum is sharded.

00:00:56.948 --> 00:00:59.775
Each GPU updates its own slice of the momentum.

00:01:00.195 --> 00:01:03.953
One exchange hands every GPU a set of complete matrices.

00:01:04.373 --> 00:01:06.150
It orthogonalises them locally.

00:01:07.082 --> 00:01:09.047
A second exchange sends the updates home.

00:01:09.047 --> 00:01:11.574
It all costs about two percent of a step.

00:01:13.192 --> 00:01:14.316
Here is the formula.

00:01:15.192 --> 00:01:18.989
Training cost is about six times active parameters, eighty billion,

00:01:19.409 --> 00:01:21.539
times training tokens, fifty trillion.

00:01:21.959 --> 00:01:24.062
That is the work the cluster has to deliver.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:31.779
Sixteen thousand GPUs, thirty-eight days, twenty-one percent utilisation, eighty-nine percent goodput.

00:01:31.779 --> 00:01:35.265
The sides agree within about five percent.

00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:40.727
Microsoft reports matching DeepSeek's base model with four to twelve times fewer

00:01:40.727 --> 00:01:43.212
FLOPs, measured on its own test sets.

00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:47.703
Imbalance became a scheduling cost, not an outage.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:58.541
To recap: fixed memory with extra rounds, Muon by resharding, and numbers that close.

00:01:58.961 --> 00:02:01.044
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:01.044 --> 00:02:01.639
I'm Pumice.

00:02:01.639 --> 00:02:01.937
Bye!
